  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{{2}^{x}}{{x}^{2}-1},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0.$\n\nThe slope at $x=0$ is\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={2}^{x}$ and $v(x)={x}^{2}-1.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{\u2032}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{\u2032}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{({2}^{x})}^{\u2032}\\cdot ({x}^{2}-1)-{2}^{x}\\cdot {({x}^{2}-1)}^{\u2032}}{{({x}^{2}-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{x}ln\u20612\\cdot ({x}^{2}-1)-{2}^{x}\\cdot 2x}{{({x}^{2}-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{x}[({x}^{2}-1)ln\u20612-2x]}{{({x}^{2}-1)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}\\,(0) & =\\frac{{2}^{0}[({0}^{2}-1)ln\u20612-2(0)]}{{({0}^{2}-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-ln\u20612}{1} \\\\  & =-ln\u20612.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at $x=0$ is $-ln\u20612.$",
